WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.268
A series circuit has one unbroken path for charge flow.

2
00:00:05.268 --> 00:00:10.536
That single-path idea is the anchor for the whole lesson.

3
00:00:10.536 --> 00:00:15.804
Because there is only one route around the loop, the same current passes through every component.

4
00:00:15.804 --> 00:00:21.072
At the same time, the supply voltage is shared across the components as energy is transferred in stages.

5
00:00:21.072 --> 00:00:26.340
If those two ideas are kept separate, series-circuit questions become much easier to read and solve.

6
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:31.608
Keep this takeaway in view: Series reasoning starts with one path and one shared current.

7
00:00:31.608 --> 00:00:36.876
Structure means one path.

8
00:00:36.876 --> 00:00:42.144
Charges have only one route around the circuit.

9
00:00:42.144 --> 00:00:47.412
Current means same everywhere.

10
00:00:47.412 --> 00:00:52.680
One path means one common current through each component.

11
00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:59.259
In the series diagram, notice that every charge must pass through the first component and then the next because there is no branch to escape into.

12
00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:05.837
That is why the current stays the same all the way around.

13
00:01:05.837 --> 00:01:12.416
But the charges transfer energy at each component, so the potential difference is shared between them.

14
00:01:12.416 --> 00:01:18.994
The diagram is doing two teaching jobs at once: it shows the structural reason for common current and the energy-transfer reason for split voltage.

15
00:01:18.994 --> 00:01:25.573
Keep this takeaway in view: The structure of the series circuit explains the rule set.

16
00:01:25.573 --> 00:01:32.151
No branches: There is no alternate path for the charge flow.

17
00:01:32.151 --> 00:01:38.730
Common current: Every component lies on the same single route.

18
00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:43.728
Imagine a long corridor with two checkpoints and no side exits.

19
00:01:43.728 --> 00:01:48.726
Every person walking through must pass both checkpoints in sequence.

20
00:01:48.726 --> 00:01:53.725
That is like current in a series circuit.

21
00:01:53.725 --> 00:01:58.723
The same flow passes each checkpoint because there is only one route.

22
00:01:58.723 --> 00:02:03.721
But the effort lost at each checkpoint can be different, which is similar to the way voltage is shared across components.

23
00:02:03.721 --> 00:02:08.719
The analogy helps because it turns the abstract circuit layout into a clear route story.

24
00:02:08.719 --> 00:02:13.717
Keep this takeaway in view: Series circuits are route stories first, then number stories.

25
00:02:13.717 --> 00:02:18.715
One corridor means series path.

26
00:02:18.715 --> 00:02:23.714
Only one route exists from start to finish.

27
00:02:23.714 --> 00:02:28.712
Same people means same current.

28
00:02:28.712 --> 00:02:33.710
The same flow passes each checkpoint in turn.

29
00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:39.323
Two formal results matter most here.

30
00:02:39.323 --> 00:02:44.935
First, total resistance in series is the sum of the individual resistances because each extra component adds more opposition along the same path.

31
00:02:44.935 --> 00:02:50.548
Second, the total supply voltage is the sum of the voltage drops across the components.

32
00:02:50.548 --> 00:02:56.161
These rules belong together because they both come from the one-loop structure.

33
00:02:56.161 --> 00:03:01.774
One path means current is common, resistances add, and voltage is shared.

34
00:03:01.774 --> 00:03:07.386
Keep this takeaway in view: Series formulas make sense once the one-path structure is visible.

35
00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:12.999
Resistance: Each extra series component adds more opposition in the same path.

36
00:03:12.999 --> 00:03:18.612
Voltage: The supply voltage is split across the components.

37
00:03:18.612 --> 00:03:24.225
R_total = R1 + R2 + ...

38
00:03:24.225 --> 00:03:29.837
V_total = V1 + V2 + ...

39
00:03:29.837 --> 00:03:35.450
Current is the same through all components in the one-loop circuit.

40
00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:41.477
When another resistor is added in series, the total resistance increases because there is now more opposition in the same single path.

41
00:03:41.477 --> 00:03:47.503
If the supply voltage stays the same, the current in the whole loop becomes smaller.

42
00:03:47.503 --> 00:03:53.530
This pattern matters because it helps learners predict the circuit behavior before calculating anything.

43
00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:59.557
Series circuits reward this kind of reasoning because the structure is simple and the consequences are direct.

44
00:03:59.557 --> 00:04:05.583
Keep this takeaway in view: In series, adding resistance affects the whole loop because everything shares one path.

45
00:04:05.583 --> 00:04:11.610
Add resistor means R_total increases.

46
00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:17.637
Extra opposition is placed in the same path.

47
00:04:17.637 --> 00:04:23.663
Same supply voltage means current decreases.

48
00:04:23.663 --> 00:04:29.690
More total resistance means a smaller circuit current.

49
00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:34.649
A very common mix-up is to import the parallel rule into a series question.

50
00:04:34.649 --> 00:04:39.608
In series, the current is the same and the voltage is shared.

51
00:04:39.608 --> 00:04:44.566
In parallel, the voltage is the same and the current splits.

52
00:04:44.566 --> 00:04:49.525
If you swap those two rule sets, the rest of the question quickly falls apart.

53
00:04:49.525 --> 00:04:54.484
This lesson is really about becoming disciplined with which circuit structure goes with which rule family.

54
00:04:54.484 --> 00:04:59.442
Keep this takeaway in view: Keep the series rule family together and do not swap in the parallel rules.

55
00:04:59.442 --> 00:05:04.401
Series: same current.

56
00:05:04.401 --> 00:05:09.360
Series: voltage is shared.

57
00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.700
Check yourself quickly.

58
00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:16.040
In a series circuit, what stays the same through each component?

59
00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:19.380
The current.

60
00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:22.720
What happens to the supply voltage?

61
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:26.060
It is shared across the components.

62
00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:29.400
And how do the resistances combine?

63
00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:32.740
They add to give the total resistance.

64
00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:36.080
Those three answers are the core of the lesson, and everything else grows from them.

65
00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:39.420
Keep this takeaway in view: One path, same current, shared voltage, added resistance.

66
00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.760
Same quantity means current.

67
00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:46.100
One path means one common current.

68
00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:49.440
Shared quantity means voltage.

69
00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.780
The supply voltage is divided across components.

70
00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:58.545
You are ready for the worked example now.

71
00:05:58.545 --> 00:06:04.311
In a series circuit, the current is the same through every component and the resistances add.

72
00:06:04.311 --> 00:06:10.076
So if one resistor is 2 ohms and the next is 4 ohms, the total resistance is 6 ohms.

73
00:06:10.076 --> 00:06:15.842
Keep the meaning attached: both resistors lie on the same single path, so their opposition combines in that one route.

74
00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:21.607
At the same time, the supply voltage would be shared across them rather than staying identical across each one.

75
00:06:21.607 --> 00:06:27.373
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: series structure first, then the calculation.

76
00:06:27.373 --> 00:06:33.138
Step 1: Identify that the circuit is series because there is one path.

77
00:06:33.138 --> 00:06:38.904
Step 2: Use the series resistance rule and add the resistor values.

78
00:06:38.904 --> 00:06:44.669
R_total = R1 + R2.

79
00:06:44.669 --> 00:06:50.435
R_total = 2 + 4.

80
00:06:50.435 --> 00:06:56.200
Use the series rule because both resistors are on one shared path.
